=== QF001 | a builtin used as a parameter
f:{[count] count+1}

=== QF001 | a q keyword from .Q.res, not just the .q namespace
f:{[sin] sin*2}

=== QF001 | qSQL keywords are reserved too
f:{[from] from}

=== clean | a name that merely resembles a builtin is fine
f:{[cnt] cnt+1}

=== clean | `find` and `fill` are operators in q, not names it reserves
f:{[find] find}

=== clean | a builtin name inside a longer name is not the builtin
f:{[counted] counted+1}

=== clean | a builtin as a namespaced field is not a local shadow
.email.subject:"hi"

=== QF002 | the drop operator cannot be a parameter
f:{[_] x+1}

=== QF004 | a definition inside a namespace that shadows a builtin
\d .util
count:{[t] 42}
\d .

=== clean | a file may end inside a namespace it opened
\d .util
helper:{[x] x}

=== clean | a later directive back to the root closes the file
\d .util
helper:{[x] x}
\d .

=== clean | the same name written dotted is not inside the namespace block
.util.count:{[t] 42}

=== QF005 | a nested lambda cannot see the enclosing local
f:{[a] g:{[b] a+b}; g[1]}

=== clean | passing the outer local in explicitly is the fix
f:{[a] g:{[a;b] a+b}; g[a;1]}

=== QF007 | a call in the parameter slot: q refuses the source outright
f:{[tables[]] x+1}

=== QF007 | an expression in the parameter slot
f:{[a+b] 1}

=== QF007 | an integer literal reads as body text, not a parameter
f:{[1] x+1}

=== QF007 | a symbol literal likewise
f:{[`s] x+1}

=== QF007 | two names without a separator is one bracket expression
f:{[a b] a}

=== QF007 | an index in the parameter slot
f:{[a[0]] a}

=== QF007 | an assignment in the parameter slot
f:{[a:1] a}

=== QF007 | a string literal, which the rules see only as blanks
f:{["s"] 1}

=== clean | a dotted name is a legal parameter, surprising as that is
f:{[.q.z] 1}

=== clean | underscores inside a name are fine
f:{[a_b] a_b+1}

=== clean | whitespace around the separators
f:{[ a ; b ] a}

=== QF010 | no space before the body does not hide the global x
f:{[a]x+1}

=== clean | the same shape where the body uses its parameter
f:{[a]a+1}

=== clean | the empty parameter list
f:{[] 42}

=== clean | implicit arguments, with no signature to contradict
f:{x+y}

=== QF008 | a parameter declared twice
f:{[a;a] a}

=== QF008 | and not only adjacently
f:{[a;b;a] a}

=== QF009 | a trailing separator is a third parameter, named `2` by q
f:{[a;b;] a}

=== QF009 | a leading one likewise
f:{[;a] a}

=== QF010 | x is a global here, not an argument: q throws 'x
f:{[a] x+1}

=== QF010 | the same for z
f:{[a] z*2}

=== clean | a global x that does exist is a legitimate reference
x:1;f:{[a] x+1}

=== clean | x declared as the parameter is the argument
f:{[x] x+1}

=== clean | a column named x in qSQL is not the implicit argument
f:{[t] select x from t}

=== QF001 | a join keyword is reserved even though it reads like a name
f:{[aj] aj}

=== QF001 | control words are reserved
f:{[while] while}

=== QF001 | several at once
f:{[count;til] count til}

=== clean | a name q does not reserve, however builtin it sounds
f:{[median] median}

=== QF012 | a reserved name assigned at the root
count:42

=== QF012 | one q silently accepts, and the linter still flags
from:1

=== QF003 | a local shadow at column zero is not root scope
f:{[x]
count:1
}

=== QF013 | a builtin column name in a table literal
t:([] first:1 2 3)

=== QF013 | the second column is checked too
t:([] px:1 2; last:3 4)

=== clean | ordinary column names
t:([] px:1 2; qty:3 4)

=== QF014 | read before the assignment that makes it local
f:{[x] r:cfg; cfg:1; r}

=== QF014 | a read in a condition, assigned in the branch
f:{[x] if[a;a:1]}

=== QF014 | amend is a read of the unset local
f:{a+:1;a:2}

=== clean | assigned first, read after
f:{[x] cfg:1; r:cfg; r}

=== clean | a parameter is set from the start
f:{[a] b:a; b:b+1; b}

=== clean | a global assignment does not make the name local
f:{[x] r:cfg; cfg::1; r}

=== clean | column names in a query resolve to the table
f:{[t] r:select from t where px>1; px:2; r}

=== QF015 | return is not a q keyword
f:{[x] return x+1}

=== QF015 | else is not either
if[a;1] else 2

=== QF015 | true and false are undefined names
f:{[x] $[x;true;false]}

=== clean | null is a q function
f:{[x] null x}

=== clean | a file that defines the name has made it one
return:{[x] x}
f:{[x] return x}

=== clean | a symbol is not a name
s:`return

=== clean | within one statement q evaluates right to left, so this assigns first
f:{[x] x#a where (a:x mod 7) in 2 3}

=== clean | a table-literal column is not an assignment
f:{[x] if[x; :([] range_from:`timestamp$())]; range_from:1}

=== clean | a parameter named like a keyword is a name
f:{[func;return] if[()~return; 1]}

=== clean | call arguments run right to left, so the later slot assigns first
f:{[x] g[c-1; c:count x]}

=== clean | two expressions side by side: the right one runs first
f:{[y] $[1=n; first; flip] $[1=n:depth y; enlist; flip] y}

=== clean | a control bracket nested in a call slot still runs left to right within itself
f:{[x] g[$[c;1;2]; c:1]}

=== QF014 | but a control bracket orders its own slots
f:{[x] g[if[c; c:1]]}

=== clean | multiple assignment binds its names
f:{[x;y] (tx;ty):type each (x;y); if[tx=11h; tx:0h]; tx+ty}

=== QF005 | the names a multiple assignment binds are locals, which a nested lambda cannot see
f:{[x] (a;b):x; g:{[v] v+a}; g 1}
